                    <h1 class="content-title">Operational Resilience <span class="gradient-text">& BCM</span></h1>
                    <p class="content-subtitle">
                        Understand how ServiceNow connects risk management with business continuity — from 
                        identifying critical business services and mapping dependencies to planning recovery 
                        strategies and managing crises in real time.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <!-- Resilience vs BCM -->
                <section class="content-section" id="resilience-vs-bcm">
                    <h2>Operational Resilience vs Business Continuity</h2>
                    <p>
                        While often used interchangeably, <strong>Operational Resilience</strong> and 
                        <strong>Business Continuity Management (BCM)</strong> serve distinct but complementary 
                        roles in protecting an organization's ability to deliver critical services.
                    </p>

                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Aspect</th>
                                    <th>Operational Resilience</th>
                                    <th>Business Continuity (BCM/BCDR)</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Focus</strong></td>
                                    <td>Proactive — ability to prevent, adapt, and recover</td>
                                    <td>Reactive — pre-planned responses and recovery procedures</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Scope</strong></td>
                                    <td>End-to-end business services across all disruption types</td>
                                    <td>Specific processes, assets, and IT systems</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Key Concept</strong></td>
                                    <td>Impact tolerances — "How much disruption can we absorb?"</td>
                                    <td>Recovery objectives — "How fast can we restore?"</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Regulatory Driver</strong></td>
                                    <td>DORA, Basel III, UK PS 6/7 & SS 1/21</td>
                                    <td>ISO 22301, SOC 2, industry-specific mandates</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>ServiceNow Module</strong></td>
                                    <td>Operational Resilience Management</td>
                                    <td>Business Continuity Management (BCM)</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                        <div class="info-card-title">The Convergence Trend</div>
                        <p>
                            Modern frameworks like <strong>DORA (Digital Operational Resilience Act)</strong> 
                            blur the line between resilience and continuity. ServiceNow's approach integrates both 
                            into the IRM suite, sharing the same CMDB foundation and risk data. This means a 
                            single risk event can simultaneously trigger a BCP activation <em>and</em> update 
                            the resilience posture of affected business services.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Critical Business Services -->
                <section class="content-section" id="critical-services">
                    <h2>Critical Business Services</h2>
                    <p>
                        The foundation of operational resilience is identifying and mapping your 
                        <strong>Critical Business Services (CBS)</strong> — the customer-facing or 
                        internally essential services that, if disrupted, would cause unacceptable harm 
                        to customers, the market, or the organization itself.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Examples of Critical Business Services</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Industry</th>
                                    <th>Critical Business Service</th>
                                    <th>Impact Tolerance</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Banking</td>
                                    <td>Online Payment Processing</td>
                                    <td>Max 2-hour disruption</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Healthcare</td>
                                    <td>Electronic Health Records Access</td>
                                    <td>Max 30-minute disruption</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Insurance</td>
                                    <td>Claims Processing</td>
                                    <td>Max 4-hour disruption</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Retail</td>
                                    <td>E-commerce Platform</td>
                                    <td>Max 1-hour disruption</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <p>
                        In ServiceNow, Critical Business Services are defined in the <strong>CMDB</strong> using 
                        the <code>cmdb_ci_service</code> table (or its child tables). They connect to the 
                        <strong>CSDM (Common Service Data Model)</strong> framework, which maps the relationship 
                        between business services, technical services, and the infrastructure supporting them.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <!-- Business Impact Analysis -->
                <section class="content-section" id="bia">
                    <h2>Business Impact Analysis (BIA)</h2>
                    <p>
                        The <strong>Business Impact Analysis</strong> is the foundational assessment that bridges 
                        risk management with continuity planning. It quantifies what happens when a critical service 
                        goes down — in terms of financial impact, customer harm, regulatory penalties, and 
                        reputational damage.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Key BIA Outputs</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Metric</th>
                                    <th>Definition</th>
                                    <th>Example</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>RTO (Recovery Time Objective)</strong></td>
                                    <td>Maximum acceptable downtime before the business impact becomes unacceptable</td>
                                    <td>Payment processing must recover within 2 hours</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>RPO (Recovery Point Objective)</strong></td>
                                    <td>Maximum acceptable data loss measured in time — how far back can you afford to roll back?</td>
                                    <td>Transaction data can lose at most 15 minutes of records</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>MTPD (Maximum Tolerable Period of Disruption)</strong></td>
                                    <td>Absolute maximum time a service can be unavailable before causing permanent damage</td>
                                    <td>Core banking: 24 hours before regulatory action</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Financial Impact</strong></td>
                                    <td>Dollar cost per hour/day of downtime including lost revenue, SLA penalties, and remediation costs</td>
                                    <td>$250K/hour for e-commerce platform outage</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">BIA to Recovery Strategy Flow</div>
                        <div class="mermaid">
                        flowchart LR
                            A["Identify Critical<br/>Business Services"] --> B["Conduct Business<br/>Impact Analysis"]
                            B --> C["Define RTO/RPO<br/>& Impact Tolerances"]
                            C --> D["Map Dependencies<br/>(CMDB)"]
