                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Dependency Mapping -->
                <section class="content-section" id="dependency-mapping">
                    <h2>CMDB Dependency Mapping</h2>
                    <p>
                        The power of ServiceNow's approach to resilience lies in its integration with the 
                        <strong>CMDB</strong>. Rather than maintaining separate spreadsheets of dependencies, 
                        the platform uses real-time CMDB relationship data to understand how infrastructure 
                        components, applications, and services are connected.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Dependency Layers</h3>
                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">Service Dependency Stack</div>
                        <div class="mermaid">
                        flowchart TB
                            CBS["Critical Business Service<br/>(Online Banking)"]
                            TS1["Technical Service<br/>(Payment API)"]
                            TS2["Technical Service<br/>(Auth Service)"]
                            APP1["Application<br/>(Payment Gateway App)"]
                            APP2["Application<br/>(Identity Provider)"]
                            INFRA1["Infrastructure<br/>(DB Cluster - Primary)"]
                            INFRA2["Infrastructure<br/>(Load Balancer)"]
                            INFRA3["Infrastructure<br/>(Auth Server)"]
                            VENDOR["Third-Party<br/>(Cloud Provider)"]

                            CBS --> TS1
                            CBS --> TS2
                            TS1 --> APP1
                            TS2 --> APP2
                            APP1 --> INFRA1
                            APP1 --> INFRA2
                            APP2 --> INFRA3
                            INFRA1 --> VENDOR
                            INFRA2 --> VENDOR
                            INFRA3 --> VENDOR

                            style CBS fill:transparent,stroke:#ef4444,color:#d1d5db
                            style TS1 fill:transparent,stroke:#f59e0b,color:#d1d5db
                            style TS2 fill:transparent,stroke:#f59e0b,color:#d1d5db
                            style APP1 fill:transparent,stroke:#3b82f6,color:#d1d5db
                            style APP2 fill:transparent,stroke:#3b82f6,color:#d1d5db
                            style INFRA1 fill:transparent,stroke:#10b981,color:#d1d5db
                            style INFRA2 fill:transparent,stroke:#10b981,color:#d1d5db
                            style INFRA3 fill:transparent,stroke:#10b981,color:#d1d5db
                            style VENDOR fill:transparent,stroke:#8b5cf6,color:#d1d5db
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <p>
                        This dependency mapping enables <strong>impact analysis</strong> — if a database cluster 
                        goes down, the system can instantly trace upward through the dependency chain to identify 
                        which business services are affected and alert the appropriate teams.
                    </p>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">CSDM Alignment</div>
                        <p>
                            For dependency mapping to work effectively, your CMDB must follow the 
                            <strong>Common Service Data Model (CSDM)</strong> framework. CSDM defines 
                            the standard layers — Business Service → Technical Service → Application → 
                            Infrastructure — and the relationships between them. Without CSDM, dependency 
                            queries return incomplete or inaccurate results.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Recovery Planning -->
                <section class="content-section" id="recovery-planning">
                    <h2>Recovery Planning & BCP</h2>
                    <p>
                        Once BIA and dependency mapping are complete, the next step is creating actionable 
                        <strong>Business Continuity Plans (BCPs)</strong> and <strong>Disaster Recovery (DR) 
                        procedures</strong> for each critical service.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Plan Components</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Recovery Teams</strong> — Defined roles and responsibilities for each phase of recovery (assessment, containment, recovery, restoration).</li>
                        <li><strong>Communication Plans</strong> — Escalation paths, stakeholder notifications, and public communication templates.</li>
                        <li><strong>Recovery Procedures</strong> — Step-by-step technical and business procedures for restoring each critical service.</li>
                        <li><strong>Resource Requirements</strong> — Pre-identified alternate facilities, backup systems, and emergency contacts.</li>
                        <li><strong>Testing Schedule</strong> — Tabletop exercises, simulations, and full failover tests to validate plan effectiveness.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h3>Recovery Strategy Selection</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Strategy</th>
                                    <th>RTO</th>
                                    <th>Cost</th>
                                    <th>Use Case</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Hot Standby</strong></td>
                                    <td>Minutes</td>
                                    <td>Highest</td>
                                    <td>Mission-critical services (payments, trading)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Warm Standby</strong></td>
                                    <td>Hours</td>
                                    <td>Medium</td>
                                    <td>Important services (email, CRM)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Cold Standby</strong></td>
                                    <td>Days</td>
                                    <td>Lowest</td>
                                    <td>Non-critical services (reporting, archives)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Cloud Failover</strong></td>
                                    <td>Minutes to Hours</td>
                                    <td>Variable</td>
                                    <td>Cloud-native services with multi-region capability</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Crisis Management -->
                <section class="content-section" id="crisis-management">
                    <h2>Crisis Management Workflows</h2>
                    <p>
                        When a disruption occurs, ServiceNow provides <strong>crisis management</strong> 
                        capabilities that coordinate the organizational response in real time.
                    </p>

                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">Crisis Management Timeline</div>
                        <div class="mermaid">
                        flowchart LR
                            A["Disruption<br/>Detected"] --> B["Assess<br/>Impact"]
                            B --> C["Activate<br/>BCP"]
                            C --> D["Notify<br/>Stakeholders"]
                            D --> E["Execute Recovery<br/>Procedures"]
                            E --> F["Monitor<br/>Progress"]
                            F --> G["Service<br/>Restored"]
                            G --> H["Post-Incident<br/>Review"]

                            style A fill:transparent,stroke:#ef4444,color:#d1d5db
                            style B fill:transparent,stroke:#f59e0b,color:#d1d5db
                            style C fill:transparent,stroke:#8b5cf6,color:#d1d5db
                            style D fill:transparent,stroke:#3b82f6,color:#d1d5db
                            style E fill:transparent,stroke:#06b6d4,color:#d1d5db
                            style F fill:transparent,stroke:#10b981,color:#d1d5db
                            style G fill:transparent,stroke:#10b981,color:#d1d5db
                            style H fill:transparent,stroke:#6b7280,color:#d1d5db
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Key Capabilities</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Automated BCP Activation</strong> — Major incidents or specific event types can automatically activate the relevant BCP and notify recovery teams.</li>
                        <li><strong>Real-Time Task Tracking</strong> — Recovery tasks are tracked in real time, with progress dashboards showing which procedures are complete and which are blocked.</li>
                        <li><strong>Stakeholder Communication</strong> — Automated notifications via email, SMS, and ServiceNow Connect for internal communication, plus pre-drafted external communications.</li>
                        <li><strong>Post-Incident Review</strong> — After recovery, the platform facilitates lessons-learned reviews, updating BCPs and resilience plans based on what was learned during the actual event.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <!-- Developer Patterns -->
                <section class="content-section" id="developer-resilience">
                    <h2>Developer Patterns</h2>
                    <p>
                        Developers working with Operational Resilience and BCM in ServiceNow commonly 
                        encounter these scenarios.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Common Development Tasks</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Task</th>
                                    <th>Approach</th>
                                    <th>Key Considerations</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Automated BIA questionnaire distribution</td>
                                    <td>Flow Designer with scheduled triggers based on BIA review cycles</td>
                                    <td>Ensure questionnaires are linked to the correct business service in CMDB</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Custom dependency impact analysis</td>
                                    <td>Script Include using <code>GlideRelatedList</code> or the Relationship API to traverse CMDB relationships</td>
                                    <td>Performance — deep dependency traversals on large CMDBs can be expensive</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>BCP activation from Major Incident</td>
                                    <td>Business Rule on <code>incident</code> table that triggers a Flow when priority = 1 and affected CI maps to a critical service</td>
                                    <td>Avoid duplicate activations — use state checks on the BCP record</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Custom resilience dashboard</td>
                                    <td>Performance Analytics with custom indicators tracking RTO compliance, BCP coverage, and test results</td>
                                    <td>Use breakdowns by business unit, service tier, and geography</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Integration with external alerting (PagerDuty, etc.)</td>
                                    <td>IntegrationHub spoke or custom webhook to trigger external alerting when BCP is activated</td>
                                    <td>Include recovery task details and stakeholder contacts in the payload</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Code Pattern: Find Affected Business Services from CI</h3>
                    <div class="code-block">
                        <div class="code-header">
                            <span class="code-lang">JavaScript — Resilience Utils Script Include</span>
                            <button class="code-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code to clipboard">Copy</button>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-body">
                            <pre><code class="language-javascript">// Script Include: ResilienceUtils
var ResilienceUtils = Class.create();
ResilienceUtils.prototype = {
    initialize: function() {},

    getAffectedBusinessServices: function(ciSysId) {
        var affectedServices = [];
        var rel = new GlideRecord('cmdb_rel_ci');
        
        // Find upstream dependencies (services that depend on this CI)
        rel.addQuery('child', ciSysId);
        rel.addQuery('type.name', 'Depends on::Used by');
        rel.query();
        
        while (rel.next()) {
            var parentCI = rel.parent.getRefRecord();
            if (parentCI.sys_class_name == 'cmdb_ci_service') {
                affectedServices.push({
                    sysId: parentCI.getUniqueValue(),
                    name: parentCI.getValue('name'),
                    isCritical: parentCI.getValue('busines_criticality') == '1'
                });
            } else {
                // Recursively check upstream
                var upstream = this.getAffectedBusinessServices(
                    parentCI.getUniqueValue()
                );
                affectedServices = affectedServices.concat(upstream);
            }
        }
        return affectedServices;
    },

    type: 'ResilienceUtils'
};</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--warning">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Performance Warning</div>
                        <p>
                            Recursive dependency traversal can be <strong>extremely expensive</strong> on 
                            large CMDBs. Always set a maximum recursion depth (e.g., 5 levels) and cache 
                            results where possible. For production use, consider using the 
                            <strong>Service Mapping</strong> or <strong>Dependency Views</strong> APIs 
                            which are optimized for this purpose.
                        </p>
                    </div>
